Say I want to a/b test an approach to working with claude code. I could
set up an a/b test harness, and do evals, and all that. There's probably
a time and a place for that.

Right now I'm just thinking of the day-to-day experience of working with
it, being able to try out options.

Consider the commit plan approach I've been mulling over. Sure, I could
commit that to CLAUDE.md - but why? I think that's more of a skill than
a global constraint, right? If there's another approach that I might try
(like spec-first) then it seems reasonable to split those up into
distinct skills. Then given a particular problem, I can try solving it
either with commit-plan or spec-first.

Research what other people are doing in this area. I want this monorepo
to support learning - and that means trying different approaches without
having to commit to them long-term.

Thinking more like a programmer, it's designing an interface. We might
have a build function in the interface, which can be implemented either
commit-plan or spec-first. The general interface stays the same "build
this new feature" but the implementation of it is swappable.
